data: {"type":"block","block":{"type":"WritingDraft","id":"turn-7b25a38ed173-writing-draft","meta":{"status":"ready","error":null},"data":{"kind":"script","title":"Retrieval in RAG: An Audio Overview","sections":[{"heading":"Opening","segments":[{"text":"A model that relies on its pre-training data can't speak to recent developments, so the survey gives the example of a user asking ChatGPT about a recent, widely discussed news story. Retrieval fills that gap by pulling in outside knowledge.","citationIds":["c1"],"speaker":"guest"},{"text":"So what has to be decided when you design the retrieval step?","citationIds":["c2"],"speaker":"host"},{"text":"The source itself has changed over time. Text was the mainstream source at first, then semi-structured data like PDFs and structured data like knowledge graphs were added, and there is a growing trend of retrieving from content generated by LLMs themselves.","citationIds":["c2"],"speaker":"guest"}]},{"heading":"Key ideas","segments":[{"text":"Is retrieval always a single lookup, or can it be more elaborate?","citationIds":["c3"],"speaker":"host"},{"text":"It can be iterative. The knowledge base is searched repeatedly using the initial query and the text generated so far, which gives a more comprehensive context for the model.","citationIds":["c3"],"speaker":"guest"},{"text":"And what about systems that decide for themselves when to retrieve?","citationIds":["c4"],"speaker":"host"},{"text":"That's adaptive retrieval, seen in techniques like FLARE and Self-RAG. The system autonomously determines whether retrieval is needed and when to stop, often using special tokens generated by the LLM for control.","citationIds":["c3"],"speaker":"guest"},{"text":"Recall is often the primary metric in typical retrieval experiments. One chunking study argues that precision and F1 matter too, because the number of retrieved chunks is fixed while the number of retrieved documents varies.","citationIds":["c6"],"speaker":"guest"}]}],"citations":[{"id":"c1","chunkId":"396a0fadeb4cd40f_c_589bc4e8cae01e02","docId":"396a0fadeb4cd40f","docTitle":"2312.10997_Retrieval_Augmented_Generation_for_Large_Language_Models_A_Survey","page":2,"quote":"RAG bridges this information gap by sourcing and incorporating knowledge from external databases."},{"id":"c2","chunkId":"396a0fadeb4cd40f_c_f22af6d01a52a8fb","docId":"396a0fadeb4cd40f","docTitle":"2312.10997_Retrieval_Augmented_Generation_for_Large_Language_Models_A_Survey","page":5,"quote":"There are several key issues involved, such as the retrieval source, retrieval granularity, pre-processing of the retrieval, and selection of the corresponding embedding model."},{"id":"c3","chunkId":"396a0fadeb4cd40f_c_18d9f568d1c19f4e","docId":"396a0fadeb4cd40f","docTitle":"2312.10997_Retrieval_Augmented_Generation_for_Large_Language_Models_A_Survey","page":10,"quote":"In addition to the most common once retrieval, RAG also includes three types of retrieval augmentation processes."},{"id":"c4","chunkId":"396a0fadeb4cd40f_c_445a807c7c2b8b50","docId":"396a0fadeb4cd40f","docTitle":"2312.10997_Retrieval_Augmented_Generation_for_Large_Language_Models_A_Survey","page":5,"quote":"This approach transcends the fixed RAG retrieval process by evaluating the necessity of retrieval based on different scenarios."},{"id":"c6","chunkId":"8bbe93a93b05967d_c_26249f4bdc41b629","docId":"8bbe93a93b05967d","docTitle":"2410.13070_Is_Semantic_Chunking_Worth_the_Computational_Cost","page":9,"quote":"In typical retrieval experiments, recall is often the primary metric."}],"bibliography":[{"docId":"396a0fadeb4cd40f","apa":"Retrieval Augmented Generation for Large Language Models A Survey. arXiv:2312.10997.","ieee":""Retrieval Augmented Generation for Large Language Models A Survey," arXiv:2312.10997.","bibtex":"@misc{arcana396a0fadeb4cd40f,
